CHECK_ROWS = [
    (1,2,"A TCP receiver has accepted bytes 100 through 199 in order. What ACK number does it send?","200",["199","100","300"],"An ACK names the next byte wanted, not the last byte received.","Bytes through 199 are complete, so byte 200 should come next."),
    (1,4,"Which combination identifies one transport endpoint?","IP address + port number + transport protocol",["MAC address + subnet mask","IP address + DNS zone","Sequence number + route metric"],"A socket endpoint tells the stack which host interface, which application, and whether TCP or UDP is being used.","192.0.2.8:443 over TCP is one endpoint."),
    (1,4,"A laptop sends a TCP packet to a web server. What does the source IP identify?","The laptop that sent this packet",["The web service inside the server","The server receiving this packet","The next router's application"],"Source means where this packet came from. The source IP names the sending device's IP location, not the program inside it.","In 192.168.1.5:52340 → 203.0.113.20:443, 192.168.1.5 is the source IP for the request."),
    (1,4,"A laptop sends a TCP packet to a web server. What does the destination IP identify?","The server this packet is trying to reach",["The browser tab that sent the request","The laptop that sent this packet","The server's HTTPS process by itself"],"Destination means where this packet is going. The destination IP names the receiving device or network interface; routers use that address to move the packet across networks.","In 192.168.1.5:52340 → 203.0.113.20:443, 203.0.113.20 is the destination IP for the request."),
    (1,4,"In 192.168.1.5:52340 → 203.0.113.20:443, what does source port 52340 identify?","The sending browser's temporary endpoint for this conversation",["The laptop's IP address","The HTTPS service waiting on the server","The physical switch port used by the laptop"],"A source port identifies the sending application endpoint. A client usually chooses a temporary port so the operating system knows which program should receive the returning data.","If several browser connections share one laptop IP, different temporary source ports keep their replies separated."),
    (1,4,"In 192.168.1.5:52340 → 203.0.113.20:443, what does destination port 443 identify?","The HTTPS service that should receive the data on the server",["The server's IP address","The browser's temporary reply endpoint","The fourth router on the path"],"The destination port identifies the receiving application or service inside the destination device. It is a software endpoint number, not a physical hole in the computer.","The server operating system sees destination TCP port 443 and gives the data to the HTTPS server process listening there."),
    (1,4,"What does TCP add to the four IP-and-port values that identify this connection?","The transport rules for a reliable, ordered, connection-oriented byte stream",["The physical cable used on every hop","The DNS name of both devices","The permanent MAC addresses of all routers"],"TCP is the protocol: the agreed set of transport rules both endpoint computers follow. TCP opens a connection, numbers bytes, acknowledges received data, retransmits missing data, restores order, and uses ports to reach the correct programs.","The same IP and port numbers used with UDP would describe a different transport conversation because UDP follows different delivery rules."),
    (1,6,"Which TCP state means the remote side closed, but the local application has not finished closing?","CLOSE_WAIT",["LISTENING","ESTABLISHED","TIME_WAIT"],"CLOSE_WAIT is local evidence that the peer's FIN arrived and the local program still needs to close its side.","A stuck application can leave many sockets in CLOSE_WAIT."),
    (1,8,"Why is the third message in the TCP handshake an ACK?","It confirms the client received the server's SYN and starting sequence number",["It assigns a DNS name","It selects a wireless channel","It translates a private IP"],"Each direction announces a starting sequence number. The final ACK confirms the server's announcement reached the client.","SYN → SYN+ACK → ACK establishes both directions."),
    (2,10,"A browser sends GET /report and receives 404 with headers and a body. Which item is the server response status?","404",["GET","/report","The browser's source port"],"The numeric status code is in the response status line. GET and /report belong to the request.","404 means the requested resource was not found."),
    (2,11,"In https://example.edu:8443/a?week=2#top, which piece is the query?","week=2",["8443","/a","top"],"The query follows ? and carries named input. The fragment follows # and is interpreted by the client.","scheme=https, host=example.edu, port=8443, path=/a, query=week=2, fragment=top."),
    (2,14,"Which protocol should a phone use to keep the same server mailbox synchronized with a laptop?","IMAP",["POP3","SMTP","FTP"],"IMAP keeps mailbox state on the server and synchronizes it. SMTP sends mail; POP3 is commonly taught as download-oriented.","Reading a message on the phone can also mark it read on the laptop through IMAP."),
    (2,17,"Which comparison is accurate?","HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP; HTTP/2 normally uses TCP",["HTTP/3 replaces DNS with ARP","HTTP/2 runs only over raw Ethernet","QUIC is a Wi-Fi channel"],"QUIC supplies secure, multiplexed transport behavior over UDP. This avoids one lost packet blocking every HTTP/3 stream at the TCP layer.","Loss can still delay the affected QUIC stream, but other streams can continue."),
    (3,21,"Several private hosts share one public IPv4 address at the same time. What must distinguish their translated flows?","Translated transport port numbers",["Different Ethernet FCS values","Different DNS root servers","Different wireless channels"],"One public IP alone cannot distinguish simultaneous TCP or UDP conversations. PAT/NAPT uses port mappings.","192.168.1.10:50000 and .11:50000 can become one public IP with different public-side ports."),
    (3,22,"Place the main DHCP lease messages in the correct order.","Discover → Offer → Request → ACK",["Offer → Discover → ACK → Request","Request → ACK → Discover → Offer","Discover → Request → Offer → ACK"],"A client first announces that it needs configuration. A server offers, the client requests an offer, and the server acknowledges the lease.","Remember DORA as a four-part exchange: the device discovers servers, receives an offer, requests that offer, and receives the final acknowledgment."),
    (3,25,"A host wants to send to an off-subnet IP. Whose MAC address does ARP resolve?","The local default gateway's interface",["The final remote server's MAC","The DNS root server's MAC","Every router's MAC at once"],"An Ethernet frame crosses one local link. For a remote destination, the next local recipient is the gateway, while the packet keeps the remote IP destination.","Each router removes the old link-layer frame and builds a new one for its next hop."),
    (3,27,"A DNS server replies, 'I do not have the final answer; ask this authoritative server next.' What kind of response is this?","An iterative referral",["A recursive final answer","A DHCP offer","An ARP cache hit"],"Iterative resolution can return the best next direction. A recursive resolver accepts responsibility for pursuing the final answer for its client.","Root and TLD servers commonly provide referrals."),
    (4,30,"A router has matching /8, /16, and /24 routes for one destination. Which route is considered first?","The /24 route",["The /8 route","Whichever was entered first","The route with the longest cable"],"Longest-prefix match selects the most specific matching destination prefix before comparing metrics among eligible alternatives.","A /24 fixes more leading destination bits than /16 or /8."),
    (4,32,"Which protocol is used primarily between autonomous systems?","BGP",["OSPF","RIP","ARP"],"BGP exchanges reachability and policy across autonomous-system boundaries. OSPF and RIP are interior protocols.","An ISP can use OSPF internally and BGP with other networks."),
    (4,34,"When can two /24 routes be summarized as one /23?","They are adjacent, correctly aligned, and share the first 23 bits",["They have the same metric only","They are anywhere in the same country","They use different first octets"],"A summary represents one exact larger binary block. Both contiguity and boundary alignment matter.","192.168.0.0/24 and 192.168.1.0/24 form 192.168.0.0/23."),
    (4,36,"In SDN, which layer actually forwards packets?","Infrastructure or data layer",["Application layer","Control layer only","DNS layer"],"Applications express policy, the controller decides and programs behavior, and forwarding devices apply the rules to packets.","A switch matches a flow entry installed by the controller."),
    (5,45,"A /24 parent is divided into /27s. How many bits were borrowed and how many subnets result?","3 borrowed bits and 8 subnets",["5 borrowed bits and 32 subnets","3 host bits and 6 subnets","27 borrowed bits and 128 subnets"],"Borrowed bits equal current prefix minus parent prefix: 27 − 24 = 3. Subnets equal 2^3 = 8.","The /27 block size is 32 addresses, producing boundaries 0, 32, 64, and so on."),
    (5,43,"What is the difference between total addresses and usable host addresses in an ordinary IPv4 subnet?","Usable hosts exclude the network and broadcast addresses",["They are always identical","Total addresses exclude both endpoints","Usable hosts count only routers"],"Every bit pattern is a total address. Ordinary usable host addresses exclude the all-host-0 network address and the all-host-1 broadcast address.","A /29 has 8 total addresses and conventionally 6 usable host addresses."),
    (5,41,"Which dotted-decimal mask is invalid because its 1 bits are not contiguous?","255.0.255.0",["255.255.224.0","255.255.255.252","255.255.128.0"],"A valid mask is all 1s from the left, followed by all 0s. Once a 0 appears, a later octet cannot return to 1s.","255.0.255.0 would have separated islands of prefix bits."),
    (5,43,"For 172.16.45.14/30, which address is the network boundary?","172.16.45.12",["172.16.45.13","172.16.45.14","172.16.45.15"],"A /30 has a block size of 4. Boundaries end in 0, 4, 8, 12, 16, and so on. Fourteen lies in the 12–15 block.",".12 is network, .13 and .14 are usable, and .15 is broadcast."),
    (6,46,"Why are channels 1, 6, and 11 commonly chosen in 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi planning?","Their commonly used 20/22 MHz channel footprints do not overlap",["They are the only legal channels everywhere","They encrypt frames automatically","They use three different MAC address sizes"],"Nearby overlapping channels can interfere. Spacing cells across 1, 6, and 11 reduces that overlap in common North American planning.","Adjacent access points should avoid occupying overlapping 2.4 GHz channels."),
    (6,49,"What does MIMO add to a Wi-Fi link?","Multiple antennas or spatial streams",["Multiple IP subnets in one frame","A wired collision detector","A DNS hierarchy"],"MIMO uses more than one radio path to carry independent data streams or improve signal robustness.","802.11n introduced MIMO to the Wi-Fi generations in this course table."),
    (6,53,"Why does Wi-Fi use collision avoidance instead of Ethernet-style collision detection?","A radio normally cannot reliably listen for a collision while it is transmitting",["Wi-Fi never shares the air","Radio signals cannot interfere","Every Wi-Fi frame uses TCP"],"A station listens first, waits, backs off randomly, transmits, and relies on a link-layer ACK to infer success.","No ACK causes a retry after another backoff."),
    (6,55,"What makes an ESS larger than a BSS?","An ESS links multiple BSS cells through a distribution system",["An ESS is one station without an AP","An ESS is a TCP state","An ESS means one Bluetooth pair"],"One BSS is one cell. Multiple connected cells can provide a larger roaming network as an ESS.","Two access points on a campus can advertise the same network as part of one ESS."),
]


